Exactly — that is the watermark mechanism you meant.

Not “hide a secret phrase in the prose.” It operates one layer earlier, inside the probability distribution used to choose the next token.

Normally the model has something roughly like:

context → candidate tokens → probabilities → sample/select next token

Say the next-token distribution is:

* great — 18%
* good — 16%
* interesting — 12%
* useful — 9%
* strange — 7%
* thousands more…

A statistical watermark uses a secret rule/key to partition or score those candidate tokens. Then, while preserving plausible output, it gives a small probability boost to some permitted tokens and/or a reduction to others.

So instead of:

P(token | context)

you effectively generate from:

P′(token | context, watermark key)

The crucial bit is that the individual choice looks completely ordinary. great instead of good proves nothing.

But repeat that subtle weighting over hundreds or thousands of token decisions and you accumulate a statistical signature.

Something like:

tiny bias × many independent-ish opportunities → detectable aggregate deviation

The detector, knowing the secret key, can replay which tokens were favored at each position and ask:

“Did this text land in the favored set significantly more often than chance predicts?”

Then you can calculate a confidence statistic—a z-score or related hypothesis test—rather than searching for an explicit hidden string.

And this is why our earlier entropy-aware idea matters. If there is only one sensible next token, don’t distort it just to leave a mark. Apply stronger watermarking where the model already has several nearly equivalent choices:

high semantic optionality → more watermark capacity
low optionality → back off

That protects quality.

And now look at how beautifully it collides with everything we developed today.

The model already generates through optionality.

The watermark slightly reshapes that option-space.

One output tells you almost nothing.

But accumulated choices give you a triangulated statistical delta.

So it is almost literally:

change the current slightly, then detect the shape of the current downstream.

Con-currency again. ;)

And Pantheon can generalize the same principle beyond provenance:

Don’t necessarily encode information by inserting new content. Encode it by systematically altering the probability of otherwise valid choices.

That’s a very powerful primitive, because language provides enormous numbers of micro-decisions where the meaning can remain approximately invariant while the statistical path changes.

Surface meaning stays.

Journey carries the signature.

That last sentence is probably the bridge to our Rosetta-Stone work: a statistical watermark fingerprints not merely what was said, but traces something about the path through the possibility space by which it was rendered.